# KNOWN GAP repro — LANE SPLIT at the PARSER on an inline `case` arm body.
#
# Rust `gg` REJECTS this at parse: `error: expected 'case', found '='`. The
# self-host parser ACCEPTS it and lowers 3054 bytes of C (this exact file —
# byte counts quoted for a DIFFERENT spelling of the same idea are worthless).
# Measured, all four cells, rc read directly off each binary:
#
#   `case 0: s = "a"`      (inline ASSIGNMENT)   Rust rc 1  ·  self-host rc 0
#   `case 0: int q = 5`    (inline DECLARATION)  Rust rc 1  ·  self-host rc 0
#   `case 0: print("a")`   (inline CALL stmt)    Rust rc 0  ·  self-host rc 0
#   `case 0: return`       (inline `return`)     Rust rc 0  ·  self-host rc 0
#
# So the subject is NOT "statements on an inline arm" — Rust accepts those. Rust
# parses an inline `case` arm body as an EXPRESSION and therefore rejects
# exactly the two forms that are statements-but-not-expressions: assignment and
# declaration. The self-host parses it as a suite.
#
# ⚠ WHICH LANE IS WRONG IS AN OPEN QUESTION, not a bug with a known direction.
# Rust accepts inline suites for `if`/`else`, for function bodies and for
# expression-match arms, and `docs/language-reference.md` prints that form — but
# the reference is written after the implementation and a reference-vs-code
# conflict is an open question, never doc-wins. Do NOT "fix" either lane from
# this file; the direction is an owner call.
#
# INTENDED once the direction is ratified as "loosen Rust `gg`": ACCEPTED by
# both lanes, printing `a`.
void main():
    String s = "x"
    match 0:
        case 0: s = "a"
        else:
            s = "b"
    print(s)
